+// g-lab-bit fork (incremental_objects.patch)
+// Experimental API.
+// Function: FPDF_SaveIncrementalObjects
+//          Writes the original file, verbatim (including any bytes before
+//          "%PDF-"), followed by ONE incremental update that contains:
+//            - the objects listed in |objnums|, in their current in-memory
+//              state (re-encrypted per object for encrypted documents), and
+//            - the objects created since the document was loaded (number
+//              above the original's last object number) that are reachable
+//              from the listed objects (or from the trailer's /Root, /Info)
+//              by following references through new objects only. Other new
+//              objects (unused fonts, appearance streams of removed
+//              annotations, ...) are not written.
+//          Nothing else is written; the /Encrypt dictionary never is. The
+//          new cross-reference section has the type of the original's last
+//          section (xref stream -> xref stream with /Type /XRef, a self entry
+//          and /W sized for the offsets; table or hybrid -> classic table)
+//          and its trailer has /Prev, /Root, /Info, /Encrypt (same value),
+//          /Size and /ID (original first element; the second is kept for
+//          encrypted files and new otherwise). Positions are relative to the
+//          "%PDF-" header, as PDFium read the original.
+//
+//          The caller MUST list every EXISTING object it modified since the
+//          document was loaded. Each call writes "original + one update"
+//          built from the in-memory state; calls on the same document do not
+//          stack. So after a first save, a second save on the same open
+//          document must list the objects of BOTH edits (cumulative since
+//          load), or the first edit is missing from the second output.
+//          Saved files are stacked by loading the saved file and saving from
+//          that document instead.
+//
+//          |file_write| must not write to the file the document was loaded
+//          from while the document is open: the original bytes are read from
+//          it during the save.
+// Parameters:
+//          document   -   Handle to a document loaded from a file or memory.
+//          file_write -   A pointer to a custom file write structure.
+//          objnums    -   Object numbers of the existing objects that
+//                         changed. Duplicates are allowed. May be NULL if
+//                         |count| is 0.
+//          count      -   Number of entries in |objnums|.
+// Return value:
+//          TRUE on success. If there is nothing to write (no objects listed
+//          and no reachable new objects), the output is an exact copy of the
+//          original and TRUE is returned.
+//          FALSE if the update cannot be written correctly. The output is
+//          then undefined (possibly partial) and must be discarded; fall back
+//          to a full save. FALSE is returned, before anything is written,
+//          when:
+//            - the document was not loaded from a file (no parser), was
+//              opened through the linearized FPDFAvail path, or PDFium had to
+//              rebuild its cross-reference table (damaged file);
+//            - a listed object does not exist, is 0, or is the /Encrypt
+//              dictionary;
+//            - ANY loaded existing object that is NOT listed references a
+//              new object: it was modified after load and its change would
+//              be lost. Examples: a /Kids array whose direct page dictionary
+//              PDFium converted to an indirect object while loading the page;
+//              with a form-fill environment, widget annotations whose
+//              appearance streams PDFium regenerated (new /AP streams
+//              referenced from the existing widget dictionaries). List those
+//              objects or fall back to a full save;
+//            - an object to write has a generation number other than 0 or
+//              references an object that has one (an indirect /Length of an
+//              unfiltered stream is exempt: it is rewritten as a direct
+//              value);
+//            - an XML metadata stream would be written unencrypted into a
+//              file whose metadata is encrypted;
+//            - the original /Size or the new xref stream's object number would
+//              exceed PDFium's maximum object number.
+//            - a classic cross-reference table would need an offset of
+//              10^10 bytes or more (the update is sized before writing).
+//          It is also returned, after writing started, on any read or write
+//          failure, including the final flush.
+FPDF_EXPORT FPDF_BOOL FPDF_CALLCONV
+FPDF_SaveIncrementalObjects(FPDF_DOCUMENT document,
+                            FPDF_FILEWRITE* file_write,
+                            const uint32_t* objnums,
+                            size_t count);
+
+// g-lab-bit fork (incremental_objects.patch)
+// Experimental API.
+// Object-number accessors for building the |objnums| list above. They use
+// uint32_t, PDFium's object number type (CPDF_Object::GetObjNum()), so the
+// values pass to FPDF_SaveIncrementalObjects() unchanged on every platform
+// (unsigned long is 32-bit on Windows but 64-bit on macOS/Linux).
+//
+// FPDFPage_GetObjectNumber: object number of the page dictionary, or 0 if
